### SKILL.md  [instructions]
- description: Finalize an AI-generated short film by checking generated shots for consistency, creating fallback clips, composing the final 1080p video with subtitles and BGM, and producing a highlight trailer. Use when the user has finished generating individual AI video shots and needs final editing, quality checks, and a trailer.
- # AI Short Film Finalization & RPA Video Generation
- ## Overview
- This skill covers the full AI short-film pipeline from **RPA-based video generation** (Google Vids / Veo 3.1, Google Flow, Gemini) through **finalization** (consistency checks, narration/subtitle sync, BGM mix, highlight trailer). Use it when the user is producing a multi-shot AI video and needs reusable automation know-how.
- The workflow has two major phases:
- 1. **RPA Video Generation**: drive the user's already-logged-in browser via Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) + Playwright to generate shots on Google Vids, Flow, or Gemini without handling OAuth inside the automation.
- 2. **Finalization**: inspect shots, replace bad ones, compose the 1080p final video with synchronized narration/subtitles/BGM, and create a highlight trailer.
- ## RPA Video Generation Workflow
- This is the most reusable part of the pipeline. The core pattern is **CDP + human-like Playwright automation**:
- - Launch Google Chrome with `--remote-debugging-port=9222` (or use an existing Chrome already running on that port).
- - Connect Playwright via `chromium.connectOverCDP('http://127.0.0.1:9222')`.
- - Reuse the user's existing Google login session, so the script does not need to handle 2FA, cookies, or OAuth tokens.
- - Use **human-like helpers** (`humanMove`, `humanClick`, `humanScroll`, `humanType`) with randomized delays/jitter to avoid simple bot detection.
- ### 1. Environment Setup
- - Install `playwright-core` (lighter than full `playwright`) in the managed Node workspace:
-   ```bash
-   cd /Users/zzhwj2026/.workbuddy/binaries/node/workspace
-   /Users/zzhwj2026/.workbuddy/binaries/node/versions/22.12.0/bin/npm install playwright-core
-   ```
- - Set `NODE_PATH=/Users/zzhwj2026/.workbuddy/binaries/node/workspace/node_modules` when running scripts, or pass it in `env` for `execSync`/`spawn` calls.
- - Verify the user's Chrome is listening on CDP: `curl http://127.0.0.1:9222/json` should return page list.
- ### 2. Google Vids (Veo 3.1) — Primary Shot Generator
- Use `generate-vids-shot-human.js` as the reusable template.
- **Key steps:**
- 1. Navigate to `https://vids.new`.
- 2. Open the Veo 3.1 panel via `button[data-view-id="getting-started-dialog-videogen"]`.
- 3. Optionally upload a reference image (Vids Ingredients) for character consistency:
-    - Click **Ingredients** button.
-    - Accept terms if a dialog appears.
-    - Set `input[type="file"]` with `setInputFiles()`.
-    - Press Escape to close the panel.
- 4. Fill the prompt into the visible textarea. Handle two placeholder variants:
-    - `"Describe your eight-second video"` (text-only)
-    - `"Specify how you want to use the ingredients"` (reference-image mode)
- 5. Capture the initial set of `<video>` srcs (inspiration gallery), then click **Generate**.
- 6. Poll for a new `<video>` src that is **not** in the initial set and **does not contain `inspirationgallery`**.
- 7. Download with `page.request.get(videoUrl)` (reuses cookies/CORS) and save to `videos/##-title.mp4`.
- **Critical parameters:**
- - `--prompt "..."`
- - `--output /path/to/shot.mp4`
- - `--ref-image /path/to/portrait.jpg` (optional, for character consistency)
- **Batch runner pattern** (`regenerate-batch-v9.js`):
- - Keep a `SHOTS` array with `id`, `title`, `prompt`, `useRef`.
- - Back up existing files before overwriting.
- - Construct `refArg` only when `useRef` is true.
- - Run `env -u NODE_OPTIONS ${NODE} ${SCRIPT} --prompt "..." --output "..." ${refArg}`.
- - Use `stdio: 'inherit'` and wrap in try/catch so one failure does not abort the whole batch.
- **Important know-how for Vids:**
- - **Reference image is not always better.** Some shots (e.g. civilian/student scenes) should use `useRef: false` to avoid the portrait's military uniform "polluting" the generation.
- - **Negative prompts are mandatory** for historical Chinese WWII shots: include `no ancient armor, no spears, no longbows, no Japanese uniforms, no samurai helmets, no peaked caps, no cap badges, no modern elements`.
- - **Positive era descriptors** for Eighth Route Army: `grey cotton uniform, flat-topped cloth cap with two plain front buttons, red scarf, modern rifle`.
- - **Prompt length**: Vids/Veo accepts long detailed prompts; prefer explicit clothing and exclusion lists over vague "historical" adjectives.
- ### 3. Google Flow — Diagnostic / Exploration Tool
- Use `diagnose-flow.js` to check project status or navigate `https://labs.google/fx/tools/flow`.
- - Connect CDP, find or navigate to the Flow page.
- - Screenshot current state to `/tmp/flow-project-list.png`.
- - List project cards via `a[href*="/flow/project/"]` or data-testid selectors.
- - Click the first project card to verify navigation.
- - Flow is mainly useful for diagnosing login state, listing available tools, and early image/video exploration. It is not the primary shot generator in this workflow.
- ### 4. Gemini — Fallback / Trailer Generator
- Use `explore-gemini.js` to probe capabilities and `generate-trailer-gemini.js` to attempt trailer generation.
- - Navigate to `https://gemini.google.com`.
- - Screenshot and inspect page text for `video`, `image`, `create`, `generate`.
- - Check for buttons: `Create image`, `Generate video`, `Create video`, or aria-label equivalents.
- - If no visible video-gen entry, fall back to typing a direct request in the chat input and sending it.
- - **Critical caveat**: Gemini video generation often requires a paid subscription (Gemini Advanced). If the feature is unavailable, **fall back immediately** to an FFmpeg highlight reel from the main video instead of blocking the delivery.
- ### 5. Character Consistency Strategy
- - Create a single `standard-portrait-v2.jpg` reference image that matches the protagonist's canonical look (e.g. round face, short black hair, grey Eighth Route Army uniform, red scarf).
- - Pass it to Vids Ingredients only for shots where the protagonist appears in military context.
- - For civilian/student/childhood shots, disable the reference image to avoid uniform leakage.
- - Document per-shot `useRef` decisions in the batch runner config.
- ## Consistency Checklist (Pre-Composition Gate)
- Run this checklist on **every shot** before final composition. Any failed check = regenerate the shot.
- ### A. Character Consistency
- | Check | Pass Criteria | Failure Example |
- | Check | Pass Criteria | Failure Example |
- | Check | Pass Criteria | Failure Example |
- | Check | Pass Criteria | Failure Example |
- |-------|--------------|-----------------|
- |-------|--------------|-----------------|
- |-------|--------------|-----------------|
- |-------|--------------|-----------------|
- | Face / identity | Same face across all shots where character appears | Different person rendered in shot 06 |
- | Hairstyle | Consistent hair length, color, style | Short hair in 01, long hair in 05 |
- | Gender | Correct gender in every shot | Male soldier rendered for female protagonist |
- | Age | Consistent apparent age (±5 years) | Child in flashback, adult in present |
- | Portrait match | If `useRef=true`, character resembles `standard-portrait-v2.jpg` | Unrelated face generated |
- | Clothing continuity | Same outfit within a continuous scene | Changed jacket mid-scene |
- ### B. Era / Period Consistency
- | Clothing era | Garments match stated historical period | Qing dynasty robe in 1940 WWII scene |
- | Weapon era | Weapons match period technology | Matchlock gun in 1940, or AK-47 in 1937 |
- | Architecture | Buildings match period and region | Concrete high-rise in 1940 village |
- | Transportation | No anachronistic vehicles | Car in 1940 rural China (unless explicitly scripted) |
- | Electronics | No anachronistic devices | Smartphone, electric light in pre-1950 scene |
- | Writing system | Correct script for era/location | Simplified Chinese characters before 1956 |
- ### C. Military / Uniform Accuracy (for war films)
- | Uniform color | Correct color for faction | Blue uniform for Eighth Route Army (should be grey) |
- | Cap style | Correct cap type | Peaked cap instead of flat-topped cloth cap |
- | Cap details | No forbidden elements | Cap badge, visor, or brim on Eighth Route Army cap |
- | Insignia | Correct or absent per era | KMT insignia on Communist soldier |
- | Weapon type | Correct for force and era | Spear as primary weapon for regular army unit |
- | Enemy depiction | Enemies NOT wearing Chinese uniforms | Japanese soldier wearing Eighth Route Army uniform |
- ### D. Background / Location Continuity
- | Season | Same season within a scene | Snow in shot 03, green leaves in shot 04 (same day) |
- | Time of day | Sun angle / light direction consistent | Morning light in wide, noon in close-up (same scene) |
- | Terrain | Location matches script | Tropical forest for northern China battlefield |
- | Sky | Overcast/clear consistent with weather in scene | Blue sky in battle scene where script says "overcast with smoke" |
- | Props continuity | Recurring props look the same | Different horse color in consecutive cavalry shots |
- ### E. Anti-Anachronism Negative Prompts (mandatory in every gen prompt)
- Every generation prompt MUST include:
- ### F. Reference Image Rules
- | Scene Type | `useRef` | Reason |
- |------------|---------|--------|
- | Military / uniformed shots | `true` | Need character consistency with uniform |
- | Civilian / student / childhood | `false` | Ref image has uniform, will pollute civilian clothing |
- | Group shots (many people) | `false` | Ref image makes everyone look like the protagonist |
- | Landscape / no character | `false` | No character to be consistent with |
- ## Post-Composition Review Workflow (v4 — Director Console)
- After the first final cut is rendered, generate a **self-contained director review console** where the human director can inspect every shot's video, audio, and subtitle **independently**, mark each component for re-make, fill in modification notes that auto-generate executable prompts, and **insert or delete shots** between existing ones.
- ### Key v4 improvements over v3
- | Feature | v3 | v4 |
- |---------|----|----|
- | Review granularity | Per-shot (single status) | **Per-component** (画面/音频/字幕 each independently pass/fix) |
- | Fix form | One combined panel | **Three separate fix forms** — video, audio, subtitle each with their own inputs |
- | Shot management | Fixed list | **Insert new shots** between any two shots; **Delete** any shot; **Undo delete** |
- | New shots | N/A | New shots get placeholder data + all three components default to "fix" |
- | Export format | `fix-list-v3.json` (flat list) | `director-fix-pack.json` with `fix_list` + `insert_list` + `delete_list` sections |
- | Problem type hints | Basic | **8 video / 5 audio / 4 subtitle problem categories** with auto-suggestions |
- | Execution commands | Per-shot | **Per-component** — each fix entry has its own `execution_cmd` |
- ### What the v4 review package contains
- For each shot / segment in the final video:
- 1. **Timestamp range** (start_ts, end_ts in the final video)
- 2. **Shot ID + title** (editable inline) + era + mood + shot type (from storyboard)
- 3. **Three independent panels** per shot:
-    - 🎬 **Video panel**: standalone .mp4 clip → screenshot → pass/review/fix toggle → fix form (problem type dropdown, director notes, auto-generated prompt, tool selector, ref-image toggle)
-    - 🔊 **Audio panel**: standalone .mp3 clip → pass/review/fix toggle → fix form (director notes, narration text override, voice selector, rate selector)
-    - 📝 **Subtitle panel**: subtitle text display → pass/review/fix toggle → fix form (director notes, subtitle text override)
- 4. **Insert zone** between every pair of shots (➕ button to add new shot)
- 5. **Delete/undo** button on each shot header
- 6. **Card border color** reflects overall shot status (green=all pass, red=any fix, orange=any review)
- 7. **Summary bar**: total duration, active shot count, pass/review/fix/deleted counters
- ### How to generate it
- This script:
- - Reads `storyboard-v2.json` and computes per-shot timestamps (cumulative from HOOK_DUR + segment durations)
- - Uses ffmpeg to extract per-shot video clips into `review-v4/clips/`
- - Uses ffmpeg to extract per-shot audio into `review-v4/audio/`
- - Extracts a representative middle frame into `review-v4/frames/`
- - Writes `review-v4/manifest.json` (machine-readable backup)
- - Reads `review-director-template.html` and replaces `__MANIFEST_DATA__` and `__PROJECT_NAME__` placeholders
- - Writes **`review-v4/review.html`** — a **fully self-contained single-file HTML page**:
-   - ✅ All data inlined as JavaScript (`const MANIFEST = [...]`) — no `fetch()` calls
-   - ✅ Works on `file://` protocol — no server needed, no CORS issues
-   - ✅ Data-driven rendering (JavaScript builds cards from MANIFEST array)
- ### Director review flow
- 1. Open `review.html` in browser or WorkBuddy preview panel
- 2. For each shot, review the three panels independently:
-    - Click ✅ 通过 / ⚠️ 待定 / ❌ 重制 on each panel
-    - When ❌ 重制 is selected, a fix form expands below that panel
- 3. To **insert a new shot**: click ➕ between any two shots (or at the end)
-    - New shot card appears with empty fields and all components set to "fix"
-    - Fill in title, narration text, subtitle text, video prompt as needed
- 4. To **delete a shot**: click 🗑 on the shot header
-    - Shot becomes greyed out; click ↩ to undo
- 5. Click **📋 导出修改包** → downloads `director-fix-pack.json`
- ### Export format: `director-fix-pack.json`
-       "tool": "vids",
-       "execution_cmd": "node generate-vids-shot-human.js --prompt '...' --output videos/06-抗日烽火.mp4 --ref-image standard-portrait-v2.jpg",
-       "video_prompt": "提示词...",
-       "video_tool": "vids",
- ### Precision re-render loop
- 1. Director reviews and exports `director-fix-pack.json`
- 2. AI reads the file and processes three lists:
-    - **fix_list**: for each entry, re-generate only the specified component (video/audio/subtitle) using `execution_cmd` or the appropriate RPA script
-    - **insert_list**: for each entry, generate new video + audio + subtitle at the specified position
-    - **delete_list**: remove the shot from the storyboard and re-compose
- 3. Update `storyboard-v2.json` with any insertions/deletions
- 4. Re-run `compose-final-v4.py` to re-compose final video
- 5. Re-run `review-final-v4.py` to regenerate review package
- 6. Director reviews the new cut
- This loop continues until the director signs off.
- ## Finalization Workflow
- 1. **Wait for batch generation tasks** to finish using TaskOutput. If a single shot hangs for more than ~10 minutes, kill it and create a fallback.
- 2. **Consistency review (pre-composition)**: inspect every generated shot against the **Consistency Checklist** below. Extract one frame every ~0.5s with ffmpeg and check:
-    - **Character consistency**: same face, hairstyle, gender, age across all shots where the character appears.
-    - **Era/period consistency**: clothing, architecture, weapons, technology match the stated historical period.
-    - **Uniform/military accuracy**: correct insignia, cap style, color for the era's military force.
-    - **Background/location continuity**: sky color, lighting direction, season, terrain consistency within a scene.
-    - **No anachronisms**: no modern objects (cars, phones, electricity) in historical scenes.
- 3. **Fix or replace bad shots**:
-    - Tighten the prompt with stronger negative constraints (no peaked caps, no badges, no Japanese uniforms, etc.).
-    - For shots that repeatedly time out, generate a fallback from a reference image using Python (PIL) + ffmpeg slow zoom.
- 4. **Run final composition** with `compose-final-v4.py` (or equivalent), which generates TTS, ASS subtitles, and 1080p final output with hook/CTA/BGM.
- 5. **Generate review package** with `review-final-v4.py`: splits the final video into per-shot segments, extracts per-segment video clips, audio clips, subtitle text, and representative frames. Outputs a **self-contained director console HTML** (data inlined, no server/CORS needed) where each shot has **three independent panels** (画面/音频/字幕), each with its own pass/fix toggle and structured fix form that auto-generates executable prompts. Supports **insert new shots** and **delete shots**. Export produces `director-fix-pack.json` with `fix_list`, `insert_list`, and `delete_list` sections.
- 6. **Precision re-render loop**: after human director marks specific segments for fix, re-generate only those shots, update the review manifest, and re-compose the final video with `compose-final-v4.py --only-shots 01,05,09`.
- 7. **Verify the final output**: check duration, resolution, and subtitle sync (last subtitle end should be within ~0.1s of audio end).
- 8. **Generate highlight trailer**. Prefer an FFmpeg reel from the main video; only attempt Gemini trailer if the user explicitly asks and the account has video generation enabled.
- ## Innovations & Lessons Learned
- ### RPA / Browser Automation
- 1. **CDP session reuse avoids authentication hell.** Instead of launching a fresh browser and fighting Google login/2FA, connect to the user's already-logged-in Chrome. The only prerequisite is `--remote-debugging-port=9222`.
- 2. **Human-like action primitives.** Randomized mouse paths, click hold durations, scroll steps, and typing delays make the script robust against simple bot detection. Abstract these into `humanMove`, `humanClick`, `humanScroll`, `humanType` and reuse everywhere.
- 3. **Detect generated output by DOM diff, not by URL pattern.** Vids inspiration-gallery videos share the page; the real generated video is a *new* `src` not in the initial set. Capture `initial` video srcs before clicking Generate.
- 4. **Use `page.request.get()` for downloads.** It automatically carries the same cookies and CORS context as the page, avoiding forbidden direct fetch and manual cookie copy.
- ### Prompt Engineering for Historical Accuracy
- 5. **Era descriptors must be explicit and repetitive.** "Chinese Communist Eighth Route Army uniform, grey cotton, flat-topped cloth cap with two plain buttons, red scarf" works better than "revolutionary soldier".
- 6. **Negative constraints are mandatory, not optional.** Every generation prompt should include the no-list: `no ancient armor, no spears, no longbows, no Japanese uniforms, no samurai helmets, no peaked caps, no cap badges, no modern elements, no smartphones, no cars`.
- 7. **Reference-image gating.** Some scenes (civilian, student, childhood) must disable the portrait reference to prevent the protagonist's military uniform from leaking into the shot.
- ### Audio / Subtitle / Rhythm Synchronization
- 8. **Subtitle ASS must be generated from the same TTS rate as the audio.** `edge-tts` SentenceBoundary offsets depend on rate. If you generate subtitles at `-5%` and audio at `+5%`, drift will exceed seconds.
- 9. **Per-shot dynamic pacing beats fixed shot length.** Forcing every shot to 8s causes freeze frames or unnatural audio stretching. Use `target_dur = audio_dur + 0.5s` and trim/slow the video accordingly.
- 10. **Never freeze the last frame.** The `tpad=stop_mode=clone` trick makes the film feel like a slideshow. Trim or slow the source video instead.
- 11. **Sample-rate consistency is non-negotiable.** `edge-tts` = 24000 Hz mono. FFmpeg `-filter_complex` resample to 44100 Hz, and the final concat must re-encode audio (`-c:a aac -ar 44100`). `-c copy` on mixed-rate inputs produces a nearly silent mix.
- 12. **When FFmpeg lacks libass/libfreetype, render subtitles as PNG overlays.** Generate transparent PNGs with PIL and burn them via `overlay` with time-based `enable='between(t\,start,end)'`. This is more reliable than fighting missing drawtext/ass filters.
- ### Tooling & Environment
- 13. **Pin managed runtimes.** Use `/Users/zzhwj2026/.workbuddy/binaries/node/versions/22.12.0/bin/node` and the isolated `playwright-core` in the managed workspace. Do not rely on global `npm` or bare `node` commands.
- 14. **Batch runners should be fault-tolerant.** One failed shot should not kill the whole batch. Wrap each shot in try/catch, log the failure, and continue.
- 15. **Gemini video generation requires subscription.** Treat it as a bonus, not a dependency. Always have a fallback FFmpeg trailer ready.
